# The composed workload for this milestone: CSV with a header row, parsed
# into a List of open-addressed HashTables (examples/stdlib/hashtable.bot's
# algorithm, unmodified), one per data row, keyed by the shared header
# Strings, all held in one outer immutable List.
#
#   name,age,city
#   Alice,31,Berlin
#   Bob,42,Hamburg
#
#     -> [ {"name": "Alice", "age": "31", "city": "Berlin"},
#          {"name": "Bob",   "age": "42", "city": "Hamburg"} ]
#
# ("{...}" above is notation for a HashTable's contents, not a Botlish
# literal -- there is no dict/record literal syntax; the actual value is a
# List of HashTable values, read back with ht_get/ht_contains.)
#
# This file exists to compose two already-working mechanisms -- CSV parsing
# (csv_geometric.bot's algorithm, duplicated verbatim below) and the
# HashTable (hashtable.bot's algorithm, duplicated verbatim below) -- and
# discover what happens when ordinary application code uses them together.
# Neither is changed here; see bench/csv_records.tcl for the allocation,
# hashing/probing and scaling measurements this composition motivates.
# Every value stays a String: no field is parsed into a typed value.
#
# Why CSV parsing and HashTable construction are each duplicated verbatim
# here rather than shared some other way: this corpus has no module/import
# system (csv_geometric.bot and csv_chunked.bot already duplicate csv.bot's
# scan_* functions for the same reason) -- every corpus file is one
# self-contained compilation unit, so composing two existing pieces means
# copying both, not inventing an include mechanism as a side effect of this
# milestone.
#
# CSV construction strategy (req #15): the GeometricBuilder variant
# (csv_geometric.bot), not the persistent list::append baseline (csv.bot,
# whose O(n^2) copying is already understood and would obscure row-table
# costs) and not the ChunkedBuilder (csv_chunked.bot) -- comparing builder
# strategies against each other is not this milestone's question, so only
# one is used, both for CSV field/record construction and, reused a third
# time, for the outer row-table List (req #5: an existing strategy, not a
# new one, and not repeated list::append).
#
# Row-shape policy (req #9), precisely:
#   empty CSV (no lines at all)        -> []
#   header row only (no data rows)     -> []
#   a data row with exactly as many fields as the header row
#                                       -> one complete row table
#   a data row with fewer fields       -> the missing trailing headers get
#                                          no key in that row's table
#   a data row with more fields        -> the declared error IndexNotFound
# This falls out of row_fill below without any dedicated validation code,
# the same way matmul.bot's "incompatible dimensions" case is an ordinary
# list::at IndexNotFound rather than a hand-written check: row_fill walks the
# row's own fields by index and looks up list::at(headers, i) for each one,
# which is exactly the header/field pairing this policy wants, and which
# itself fails with IndexNotFound the moment i reaches the header count --
# "more fields than headers" and "list::at past the end of headers" are the
# same condition, so no separate error path exists to keep in sync with it.
# csv_records declares it (`errors IndexNotFound`), so its caller decides
# what a malformed row means.
#
# Duplicate headers (req #10): ordinary HashTable replacement semantics.
# ht_set(table, "name", ...) twice, on two occurrences of "name" in the
# header row, is two calls with the same key text: the second replaces the
# first, so the row table ends up with one "name" entry holding the later
# field's value, and row width (req #46/#47's "str::length(headers)") still
# counts the header row's field count, not the table's eventual distinct-key
# count -- a presized table for a row with duplicate headers is presized
# for at least as many entries as it will ever hold, never too few.
#
# Empty header names (req #11) and empty field values are ordinary Strings
# like any other and need no special case: "" hashes and compares like any
# other key/value this table already supports.
#
# Header sharing (req #3, #16, #45): headers is list::at(records, 0),
# computed once by csv_records_generic below and passed unchanged into every
# row_table/row_fill call; list::at never copies the String it returns, so
# every row's ht_set(table, list::at(headers, i), ...) inserts the identical
# header String object build_rows started with, not a fresh copy -- the
# workload therefore exercises repeated hash()/== of the same header String
# objects across rows, on purpose (req #3), with no cached hash or
# memoization added to make that repetition cheaper (req #4, #32: that
# absence is the point -- see bench/csv_records.tcl's hash-call counts).
# Likewise every field String stored in a row table is exactly the String
# scan_field/scan_quoted produced while parsing (req #16): row_fill never
# reconstructs a value merely to store it.
#
# Default vs presized row tables (req #7, #46-#48): csv_records builds every
# row table with plain ht_new() (the general baseline, which does not know
# how many entries the row will hold); csv_records_presized builds every row
# table with ht_new_sized(header_count) instead, using only
# list::length(headers) -- information already at hand from the parsed CSV,
# not benchmark-specific knowledge. ht_new_sized already exists
# (hashtable.bot) and needed no change for this: hashtable.bot's own
# pre-sizing API was already exactly "expected entry count", not raw
# capacity, so this milestone adds no new HashTable API at all.
#
# expect: [2, "Alice", "31", "Berlin", "Bob", "Hamburg", true]

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GeometricBuilder (see csv_geometric.bot for the fuller description): a
# contiguous, geometrically-growing MutableArray[T], its storage and length
# threaded as two separate values. Used three times below -- for a record's
# fields, for the file's records, and for the outer row-table result -- with
# no change to the strategy itself, and with one untyped source
# implementation serving all three element types.

import list
import mutable_array
import str

fn geo_new(first):
    mutable_array::create(1, first)

# The capacity a builder holding LENGTH elements should grow CAPACITY to, to
# fit one more: double, or exactly enough if doubling still isn't enough
# (starting from capacity 0). This is the one place the growth policy is
# decided; MutableArray itself has no notion of growing.
fn geo_new_capacity(capacity, length):
    doubled = capacity * 2
    if doubled <= length:
        return length + 1

    doubled

# STORAGE, grown if it has no room for one more element at LENGTH: a fresh,
# larger MutableArray with STORAGE's first LENGTH elements copied in, or
# STORAGE unchanged if it already has room. VALUE, the element about to be
# appended, initializes every slot of the fresh array (mutable_array::create
# fills all of them); the slots at LENGTH and beyond are overwritten by later
# appends before freeze(LENGTH) can ever expose them.
fn geo_grow(storage, length, value) errors UpperOverrun:
    capacity = mutable_array::capacity(storage)
    if length < capacity:
        return storage

    new_capacity = geo_new_capacity(capacity, length)
    fresh = mutable_array::create(new_capacity, value)
    mutable_array::copy(fresh, 0, storage, 0, length)
    fresh

# STORAGE with VALUE appended at index LENGTH, grown first when full. The
# storage is mutated (or replaced by a grown copy, which is returned); the
# caller's new length is LENGTH + 1.
fn geo_append(storage, length, value) errors IndexNotFound, UpperOverrun:
    grown = geo_grow(storage, length, value)
    mutable_array::set(grown, length, value)
    grown

# STORAGE's first LENGTH elements as an ordinary immutable List
# (finalization: a single exact-size copy, see mutable_array::freeze).
fn geo_finish(storage, length) errors LowerUnderrun, UpperOverrun:
    mutable_array::freeze(storage, length)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CSV parsing (see csv.bot for the algorithm's description; identical to
# csv_geometric.bot, unchanged).

# Scanning hands back anonymous structs, not positional pairs: scan_field
# returns {field, index} and scan_record(_rest) returns {fields, stop}
# (STRUCTS.md), so what the scan appends is known to be a str / List[str].
# That precision is what makes the two declared builder contracts below
# necessary: scan_record_rest's `fields: MutableArray[str]` and
# scan_records's `records: MutableArray[List[str]]`. An untyped function
# that appends a value it computes itself (here scanned.field) to storage it
# received as a parameter has no definition-level proof that the storage
# holds that element type (TYPED-MUTARRAY-BUILDER-REFACTOR.md, "Definition-
# level obstruction"); while the scanned values were `any`, the storage was
# MutableArray[any] and needed none.

fn peek(text, index) errors LowerUnderrun:
    if index >= str::length(text):
        return ""

    str::substring(text, index, index + 1)

fn scan_unquoted(text, start, index) errors LowerUnderrun, UpperOverrun:
    character = peek(text, index)
    if character == "" or character == "," or character == "\n":
        return {field: str::substring(text, start, index), index: index}

    scan_unquoted(text, start, index + 1)

fn scan_quoted(text, index, field) errors LowerUnderrun:
    character = peek(text, index)
    if character == "\"":
        if peek(text, index + 1) == "\"":
            return scan_quoted(text, index + 2, str::concat(field, "\""))

        return {field: field, index: index + 1}

    scan_quoted(text, index + 1, str::concat(field, character))

fn scan_field(text, index) errors LowerUnderrun, UpperOverrun:
    if peek(text, index) == "\"":
        return scan_quoted(text, index + 1, "")

    scan_unquoted(text, index, index)

# The rest of a record whose first COUNT fields are in FIELDS, the last field
# ending at STOP. (Defined before scan_record, its only caller, like every
# helper in this file: a binding is visible only after it is defined, so a
# caller placed *before* its callee is rejected -- STRICT-REFERENCE-
# DETERMINISM.md. It used to compile, with generic, guarded copies of the
# whole call chain -- TYPED-MUTARRAY-BUILDER-REFACTOR.md, "Definition-order
# sensitivity".)
fn scan_record_rest(text, stop, fields: MutableArray[str], count) errors IndexNotFound, LowerUnderrun, UpperOverrun:
    delimiter = peek(text, stop)
    if delimiter == ",":
        scanned = scan_field(text, stop + 1)
        more = geo_append(fields, count, scanned.field)
        return scan_record_rest(text, scanned.index, more, count + 1)

    if delimiter == "\n":
        return {fields: geo_finish(fields, count), stop: stop + 1}

    {fields: geo_finish(fields, count), stop: stop}

# The record starting at INDEX and the index where it stops: the first field
# (a record always has one, possibly empty) starts the FIELDS builder.
fn scan_record(text, index) errors IndexNotFound, LowerUnderrun, UpperOverrun:
    scanned = scan_field(text, index)
    scan_record_rest(text, scanned.index, geo_new(scanned.field), 1)

# The records from INDEX on, the first COUNT records already in RECORDS.
fn scan_records(text, index, records: MutableArray[List[str]], count) errors IndexNotFound, LowerUnderrun, UpperOverrun:
    if index >= str::length(text):
        return geo_finish(records, count)

    scanned = scan_record(text, index)
    more = geo_append(records, count, scanned.fields)
    scan_records(text, scanned.stop, more, count + 1)

fn csv_parse(text) errors IndexNotFound, LowerUnderrun, UpperOverrun:
    if str::length(text) == 0:
        return []

    scanned = scan_record(text, 0)
    scan_records(text, scanned.stop, geo_new(scanned.fields), 1)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HashTable (see hashtable.bot for the algorithm's description; identical
# -- including ht_new_sized, this milestone's presizing API, which already
# existed -- up to ht_set: a table is a value, and ht_set returns the
# updated table, MUTABLE-ARRAY.md).

struct HashTable:
    controls: MutableArray[int]
    keys: mutarray
    values: mutarray
    counts: mutarray

fn ht_min_capacity():
    8

fn ht_empty_state():
    0

fn ht_occupied_state():
    1

fn ht_tombstone_state():
    2

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Construction

fn ht_alloc(capacity) errors IndexNotFound:
    counts = mutable_array::allocate(2)
    mutable_array::set(counts, 0, 0)
    mutable_array::set(counts, 1, 0)
    HashTable {controls: mutable_array::create(capacity, ht_empty_state()), keys: mutable_array::allocate(capacity), values: mutable_array::allocate(capacity), counts: counts}

fn ht_new() errors IndexNotFound:
    ht_alloc(ht_min_capacity())

# The smallest capacity, doubling from ht_min_capacity(), that keeps EXPECTED
# entries under the 2/3 load-factor policy with zero tombstones -- for a
# caller (e.g. the future CSV-header workload) that knows its entry count in
# advance and wants to avoid growing while filling the table.
fn ht_capacity_for(expected, capacity):
    if expected * 3 <= capacity * 2:
        return capacity

    ht_capacity_for(expected, capacity * 2)

fn ht_new_sized(expected) errors IndexNotFound:
    ht_alloc(ht_capacity_for(expected, ht_min_capacity()))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Fields

fn ht_size(table) errors IndexNotFound:
    mutable_array::at(table.counts, 0)

fn ht_tombstones(table) errors IndexNotFound:
    mutable_array::at(table.counts, 1)

fn ht_capacity(table):
    mutable_array::capacity(table.controls)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Probing (ordinary linear probing: see the file header)

fn ht_probe_start(table, key):
    mod(hash(key), ht_capacity(table))

fn ht_probe_next(table, index):
    mod(index + 1, ht_capacity(table))

# The index of KEY from INDEX on, or -1 if probing reaches an EMPTY slot
# first. Zero managed allocations: only mutable_array::at, ==, and mod/hash
# on values already in hand (req #10, #19).
fn ht_find_get(table, key, index) errors IndexNotFound:
    state = mutable_array::at(table.controls, index)
    if state == ht_empty_state():
        return -1

    if state == ht_occupied_state() and mutable_array::at(table.keys, index) == key:
        return index

    ht_find_get(table, key, ht_probe_next(table, index))

# The slot to use for inserting/replacing KEY from INDEX on: an existing
# equal key's own slot (replace), the first EMPTY slot (confirming the key
# is not later in the chain first), or an earlier TOMBSTONE reused once
# that confirmation is done (FIRST-TOMBSTONE is -1 until one is seen).
fn ht_find_insert(table, key, index, first_tombstone) errors IndexNotFound:
    state = mutable_array::at(table.controls, index)
    if state == ht_empty_state():
        if first_tombstone >= 0:
            return first_tombstone

        return index

    if state == ht_occupied_state() and mutable_array::at(table.keys, index) == key:
        return index

    if state == ht_tombstone_state() and first_tombstone < 0:
        return ht_find_insert(table, key, ht_probe_next(table, index), index)

    ht_find_insert(table, key, ht_probe_next(table, index), first_tombstone)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Lookup

# KEY's value, or unit if absent (see the file header's absence convention).
fn ht_get(table, key) errors IndexNotFound:
    index = ht_find_get(table, key, ht_probe_start(table, key))
    if index < 0:
        return unit

    mutable_array::at(table.values, index)

fn ht_contains(table, key) errors IndexNotFound:
    ht_find_get(table, key, ht_probe_start(table, key)) >= 0

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Resize/rehash: a fresh EMPTY destination, so the insertion routine below
# never has to consider tombstones or existing keys (req #16) -- distinct
# from, and simpler than, ht_find_insert above.

fn ht_rehash_probe(newControls, index, capacity) errors IndexNotFound:
    state = mutable_array::at(newControls, index)
    if state == ht_empty_state():
        return index

    ht_rehash_probe(newControls, mod(index + 1, capacity), capacity)

# DEST, the new table, with KEY/VALUE inserted at its first EMPTY slot from
# KEY's hash on.
fn ht_rehash_insert(dest, capacity, key, value) errors IndexNotFound:
    index = ht_rehash_probe(dest.controls, mod(hash(key), capacity), capacity)
    mutable_array::set(dest.controls, index, ht_occupied_state())
    mutable_array::set(dest.keys, index, key)
    mutable_array::set(dest.values, index, value)
    dest

# DEST with every OCCUPIED slot of the old table (its arrays CONTROLS, KEYS
# and VALUES) from I on rehashed into it at NEWCAPACITY. Only individual
# mutable_array::at/::set ever move an entry here -- no mutable_array::copy
# (req #23). (The old table's arrays are passed apart, not as the struct,
# so that an instance of this function never has to project a field of a
# table its generic caller cannot type.)
fn ht_rehash_scan(controls, keys, values, i, oldCapacity, dest, newCapacity) errors IndexNotFound:
    if i >= oldCapacity:
        return dest

    if mutable_array::at(controls, i) == ht_occupied_state():
        filled = ht_rehash_insert(dest, newCapacity, mutable_array::at(keys, i), mutable_array::at(values, i))
        return ht_rehash_scan(controls, keys, values, i + 1, oldCapacity, filled, newCapacity)

    ht_rehash_scan(controls, keys, values, i + 1, oldCapacity, dest, newCapacity)

# A fresh NEWCAPACITY table holding every currently OCCUPIED entry of TABLE
# and no tombstones. Its 4 MutableArrays are this call's only allocations
# (req #20, #41).
fn ht_rehash(table, newCapacity) errors IndexNotFound:
    dest = ht_alloc(newCapacity)
    mutable_array::set(dest.counts, 0, ht_size(table))
    ht_rehash_scan(table.controls, table.keys, table.values, 0, ht_capacity(table), dest, newCapacity)

# Whether the *next* insert (one more occupied slot) would push occupancy
# past 2/3 of capacity -- checked before insert ever writes anything.
fn ht_should_grow(table) errors IndexNotFound:
    occupied = ht_size(table) + ht_tombstones(table)
    (occupied + 1) * 3 > ht_capacity(table) * 2

fn ht_grow_or_clean(table) errors IndexNotFound:
    if ht_tombstones(table) > ht_size(table):
        return ht_rehash(table, ht_capacity(table))

    ht_rehash(table, ht_capacity(table) * 2)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Insert / replace
#
# ht_find_insert runs once against TABLE as it stands; only if that proves
# KEY is new does load factor get checked at all, so replacing an existing
# key never resizes (req #44) and never re-probes. A plain insert with
# spare capacity likewise probes exactly once. Only the (rarer, already
# allocating) grow path pays a second probe, against the freshly resized
# table -- req #19's zero-allocation targets (replace, insert with spare
# capacity, delete) never pay it.

# TABLE with the write for a confirmed-absent KEY at INDEX (an EMPTY or
# TOMBSTONE slot ht_find_insert chose): bumps size, and tombstones only if
# INDEX was reusing one. Split out of ht_set below because it is needed
# from two branches (with and without a resize in between).
fn ht_place(table, index, key, value) errors IndexNotFound:
    prior = mutable_array::at(table.controls, index)
    mutable_array::set(table.controls, index, ht_occupied_state())
    mutable_array::set(table.keys, index, key)
    mutable_array::set(table.values, index, value)
    mutable_array::set(table.counts, 0, ht_size(table) + 1)
    if prior == ht_tombstone_state():
        mutable_array::set(table.counts, 1, ht_tombstones(table) - 1)

    table

# TABLE with KEY mapped to VALUE (replacing an existing mapping of KEY).
fn ht_set(table, key, value) errors IndexNotFound:
    index = ht_find_insert(table, key, ht_probe_start(table, key), -1)
    if mutable_array::at(table.controls, index) == ht_occupied_state():
        mutable_array::set(table.values, index, value)
        return table

    if ht_should_grow(table):
        grown = ht_grow_or_clean(table)
        slot = ht_find_insert(grown, key, ht_probe_start(grown, key), -1)
        return ht_place(grown, slot, key, value)

    ht_place(table, index, key, value)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Row-table construction: the new work this milestone composes on top of the
# two unmodified pieces above.

# TABLE, freshly built to hold up to HEADER_COUNT entries: presized
# (ht_new_sized) if PRESIZE, otherwise the plain default-capacity ht_new().
fn row_new(presize, header_count) errors IndexNotFound:
    if presize:
        return ht_new_sized(header_count)

    ht_new()

# TABLE with headers[0..FIELD_COUNT) mapped to fields[0..FIELD_COUNT)
# (FIELD_COUNT is this row's own field count, list::length(fields), not the
# header count): see the file header's row-shape policy. list::at(headers,
# i) is the only bounds check this needs -- it fails with IndexNotFound by
# itself once i reaches the header count, which is exactly "more fields than
# headers".
fn row_fill(table, headers, fields, i, field_count) errors IndexNotFound:
    if i >= field_count:
        return table

    filled = ht_set(table, list::at(headers, i), list::at(fields, i))
    row_fill(filled, headers, fields, i + 1, field_count)

# One row's HashTable: ordinary HashTable APIs only (ht_new/ht_new_sized,
# ht_set), no bulk-row-insert primitive and no CSV-specific table API.
fn row_table(headers, header_count, fields, presize) errors IndexNotFound:
    table = row_new(presize, header_count)
    row_fill(table, headers, fields, 0, list::length(fields))

# RECORDS[i..) (RECORDS[0] is the header row, already consumed by the
# caller) as row HashTables, appended to the outer GeometricBuilder OUTER
# (storage) holding COUNT row tables so far -- req #5's "one existing
# efficient construction strategy" for the result, not repeated list::append.
#
# OUTER's declared MutableArray[HashTable] is this file's one concrete
# (non-generic) parameter contract on the builder path: build_rows appends a
# value it computes itself (TABLE, statically a HashTable), so its own
# definition must already know OUTER holds HashTables -- an untyped OUTER is
# analyzed as a raw mutarray of unknown elements, and geo_grow would then
# copy those unknown slots into a MutableArray[HashTable]. The geo_*
# functions themselves stay untyped. (REFACTOR-FINDING: see
# TYPED-MUTARRAY-BUILDER-REFACTOR.md, "Definition-level obstruction"; a
# table was one MutableArray, declared MutableArray[mutarray], until
# MUTABLE-ARRAY.md made it a struct of arrays.)
fn build_rows(records, i, headers, header_count, outer: MutableArray[HashTable], count, presize) errors IndexNotFound, LowerUnderrun, UpperOverrun:
    if i >= list::length(records):
        return geo_finish(outer, count)

    table = row_table(headers, header_count, list::at(records, i), presize)
    build_rows(records, i + 1, headers, header_count, geo_append(outer, count, table), count + 1, presize)

# CSV TEXT, with a header row, as an immutable List of mutable HashTables:
# one per data row, keyed by the header row's own String objects (shared,
# not reconstructed, across every row -- see the file header). Empty input
# and header-only input both produce [] (req #9); otherwise the first row
# table starts the outer builder.
fn csv_records_generic(text, presize) errors IndexNotFound, LowerUnderrun, UpperOverrun:
    records = csv_parse(text)
    if list::length(records) <= 1:
        return []

    headers = list::at(records, 0)
    header_count = list::length(headers)
    first = row_table(headers, header_count, list::at(records, 1), presize)
    build_rows(records, 2, headers, header_count, geo_new(first), 1, presize)

# The general baseline: every row table starts at default capacity, not
# knowing how many fields it will hold (req #46).
fn csv_records(text) errors IndexNotFound, LowerUnderrun, UpperOverrun:
    csv_records_generic(text, false)

# The presized variant: every row table is built for exactly this row's
# header count via ht_new_sized (req #47) -- str::length(headers), nothing else.
fn csv_records_presized(text) errors IndexNotFound, LowerUnderrun, UpperOverrun:
    csv_records_generic(text, true)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Sample: the milestone's own worked example. Both variants must agree
# (checked directly, alongside the differential/property tests in
# tests/native-csv-records.test).

fn sample_checks() errors IndexNotFound, LowerUnderrun, UpperOverrun:
    rows = csv_records("name,age,city\nAlice,31,Berlin\nBob,42,Hamburg\n")
    presized_rows = csv_records_presized("name,age,city\nAlice,31,Berlin\nBob,42,Hamburg\n")
    first = list::at(rows, 0)
    second = list::at(rows, 1)
    presized_first = list::at(presized_rows, 0)
    agree = ht_get(first, "name") == ht_get(presized_first, "name") and ht_size(first) == ht_size(presized_first)
    [list::length(rows), ht_get(first, "name"), ht_get(first, "age"), ht_get(first, "city"), ht_get(second, "name"), ht_get(second, "city"), agree]

# The sample's input is well formed, so IndexNotFound (a row with more fields
# than headers, or a broken table invariant: hashtable.bot's sample) and
# LowerUnderrun/UpperOverrun (a slice or builder range the scanners cannot
# prove in range) cannot happen; the handlers state what they would mean.
fn sample():
    checks = sample_checks():
        on IndexNotFound:
            []
        on LowerUnderrun:
            []
        on UpperOverrun:
            []
    checks

sample()
